{
  "_divergences": [
    {
      "reason": "Phase 8 Part 2 adds edge_taken tracking: new AgentTrace rows (agent_name 'resolve' and, where a human decision was involved, 'await_human') recording which edge the graph took at each branch point, plus edge_taken populated on the existing ingest/validate/explore/narrate traces. This inserts new entries into agent_trace_sequence for most scenarios that pass through resolve_node. Separately, the ingest node now writes a trace on the connector-fetch failure path (see 'connector_fetch_exception', whose recorded sequence was the empty list) so GET /audit/{run_id} can answer 'why did this run fail' even when fetch() raised before anything else was traced. Both changes are PURE INSERTIONS - every originally-recorded trace step still appears, in the same relative order - verified by tests/golden_compare.py's subsequence check rather than exact-sequence equality. No domain field (run_status, fix_chain, events, baseline_active, exploration_present, report_present, report_generation_mode) is expected to change for any scenario; those remain exact-equality checks."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Phase 8 Part 1 (graph migration): the pre-migration router built the 'ingestion' AgentTrace as the LAST step of a single monolithic function (after validate/correlate/diagnose/gate had all already run), even though ingestion is causally the FIRST thing that happens. The graph instead has a dedicated ingest_node that writes its own trace immediately as it executes, before validate_node/resolve_node ever run - so agent_trace_sequence now starts with 'ingestion' for every scenario that re-ingests against an existing baseline (previously it appeared wherever the old code happened to construct the response). This is a deliberate improvement, not a bug: the trace order now matches true execution order, which is exactly what an audit trail is for. Affected scenarios (ingestion moved to the front, all other steps keep their original relative order - a pure reordering of one element, never a removal): corruption_change_dtype, corruption_change_dtype_approve_fails_verification, corruption_drop_column, corruption_drop_column_reject_data, corruption_inject_nulls, corruption_inject_nulls_reject_fix, corruption_inject_whitespace_case, corruption_rename_column, corruption_shift_distribution, corruption_shift_distribution_accept_as_baseline, corruption_truncate_rows, corruption_truncate_rows_left_pending, low_confidence_rename_then_approved, second_clean_ingest_noop. 'baseline_sanity_rejection' is NOT in this list - its ingestion trace was already written after 'baseline_profiler' within the same node, in the same relative order as before, so no divergence applies there."
    }
  ],
